      "title": "4.3 Threat Modeling (STRIDE & PASTA)",
      "duration": "50 mins",
      "is_mandatory": true,
      "content": "## Structured Threat Modeling: Anticipating Failures by Design\n\n| - **Columns of a Professional Risk Register:** Risk ID | Asset | Risk Scenario | Threat Actor | Vulnerability | Likelihood (1-5) | Impact (1-5) | Risk Score | Current Controls | Residual Risk | Treatment Plan | Owner | Review Date | Status |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| R-001 | Customer DB | Ransomware via VPN | FIN7 | CVE-2023-XXXX, No MFA | 4 | 5 | 20 (Critical) | VPN Patch Q2, AV | High | Implement MFA, Segmentation | DB Admin | 2026-12-01 | In Progress |\n- **Inherent Risk:** The score before current controls (e.g., 25/25).\n- **Residual Risk:** The score after current controls (e.g., 20/25).\n- **Target Risk:** The score you want after treatment (e.g., 5/25).\n\n## Traceability Architecture: Linking High-Risk Scenarios Directly to Controls\n\n![Diagram 4: 4.3 Threat Modeling (STRIDE & PASTA)](/assets/courses/cyber-01/module4lesson3photo4.jpg)\n\nThis is the most practical part of the lesson. You do not pick controls because they are “best practice.” You pick controls because they treat a specific risk in your register.\n\n- **The Traceability Chain:** Risk Register Entry: “R-001: Ransomware on Customer DB via VPN. Residual Risk: Critical.”\nTreatment Decision: “Mitigate by implementing MFA on VPN and Network Segmentation.”\n- **Control Selection:** NIST 800-53 Control IA-2 (Multi-Factor Authentication) and SC-7 (Boundary Protection / Segmentation).\n- **Implementation:** Deploy Duo MFA on VPN. Configure firewall rules to segment DB subnet.\n- **Verification:** Pen test the VPN. Scan the segmentation.\nRegister Update: “R-001 Residual Risk updated from Critical (20) to Medium (8). Control IA-2 Implemented. Evidence: Screenshot of Duo Admin Console.”\n\nThis traceability (Risk → Control → Evidence) is what auditors look for. It is what the CEO looks for. It proves you are not buying tools randomly; you are solving problems.\n\n## Critical Misconceptions in Risk Documentation\n\n### Misconception 1: Confusing the Framework with the Tool\n\nBuying a GRC (Governance, Risk, Compliance) tool like RSA Archer or ServiceNow GRC does not give you a risk management program. It gives you a database. The program is the process and the people.\n\n### Misconception 2: Doing the Assessment Once a Year for the Auditor\n\n“We do our risk assessment in March for the ISO audit.” Wrong. You do it continuously. The March version is just a snapshot. If a new critical vulnerability drops in April, you reassess in April.\n\n### Misconception 3: Rating Everything “High\n\n” If everything is Critical, nothing is Critical. You must have a distribution. Use the matrix to force ranking. If you have 50 Critical risks, you have a prioritisation problem, not a risk problem.\n\n### Misconception 4: Ignoring Residual Risk\n\nYou implement a control (e.g., WAF). You mark the risk “Closed.” The risk is not closed. The residual risk remains. A WAF can be bypassed. You must monitor the residual risk.\n\n### Misconception 5: Writing Risk Scenarios in Technical Language\n\n“Risk: CVE-2023-1234 on Server X.” The board cannot read that. Write: “Risk: Attacker exploits known vulnerability on Payment Server to steal credit cards, resulting in ₹10 Cr fines and loss of PCI license.”\n\n## Chapter Synthesis and Definitional Glossary\n\nRisk assessment frameworks (NIST RMF, ISO 27001, FAIR) provide the structure to turn technical findings into business decisions. The universal process is: Context → Identification → Analysis (Qual/Quant/Semi-Quant) → Evaluation → Treatment → Monitoring. The Risk Register is the central artifact linking Assets, Threats, Vulnerabilities, Controls, and Residual Risk. Every control must trace back to a risk it treats.\n\n### Glossary of 10 terms:\n\n- **Risk Appetite**: the amount of risk an organisation is willing to accept.\n\n- **Risk Register**: the master document recording all identified risks and their status.\n\n- **Inherent Risk**: risk level before any controls are applied.\n\n- **Residual Risk**: risk level after current controls are applied.\n\n- **Risk Treatment**: the decision to mitigate, transfer, avoid, or accept.\n\n- **NIST RMF**: the 7-step US federal risk management lifecycle.\n\n- **ISO 27001**: the international standard for an Information Security Management System (ISMS).\n\n- **FAIR**: a quantitative risk model calculating financial loss exposure.\n\n- **Statement of Applicability (SoA)**: the ISO 27001 document listing which controls apply.\n\n- **Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE)**: the expected yearly financial loss from a risk.\n\n- **Risk Owner**: the business person accountable for a specific risk.\n\n## Knowledge Verification: Review Questions\n\n1. What is the difference between NIST RMF and ISO 27001 in philosophy?\n\n2. Why is “High/Medium/Low” (Qualitative) often insufficient for board reporting?\n\n3. If you implement a control but do not update the Residual Risk in the register, what is the audit finding?\n\n4. Explain the Traceability Chain: Risk → Control → Evidence.\n\n5. In your lab exercise (LAB-R-001), why was “Accept” the correct treatment decision?",
